WEBVTT

00:00:01.330 --> 00:00:08.010
Hi, this is Dr. Jelena Trajkovic, CECS Department, Cal State Long Beach. Next topic that we are

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:17.090
going to talk about are timing diagrams. In general, timing diagram will show us how do

00:00:17.090 --> 00:00:27.230
outputs or how does an output change over time. And we here have some generic inputs but you

00:00:27.230 --> 00:00:39.110
might get values on the input signals at any given time to use in problems like this.

00:00:39.110 --> 00:00:45.950
So we are going to first start with two circuits that we already learned. Basically they have

00:00:45.950 --> 00:00:56.070
only one gate and that is, as you see here on the left hand side, that is an end gate.

00:00:56.070 --> 00:01:06.610
So I gave values for a between t is equal to 0 to t is equal to 10 to be equal to 0,

00:01:06.610 --> 00:01:14.110
b is equal to 0 as well, and then in this next time interval from 10 to 20, a is equal

00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:17.750
to 1, b is equal to 0, and so on and so forth.

00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:23.430
What we need to do is we need to break down each interval, I mean the timing diagram

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:32.350
into each interval and observe the inputs. So we have 0 and 0. That is going to create

00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:47.310
output 0. 0 and 1. That is going to create output 0. 0 and 1. Another 0 and 1 and 1.

00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:54.870
Finally we got one. It is important that while you are drawing these

00:01:54.870 --> 00:02:01.430
diagrams that you very very clearly annotate and show which level is 0 and

00:02:01.430 --> 00:02:08.070
which level is 1. They need to be clearly pointed out. Second example is going to

00:02:08.070 --> 00:02:15.950
be OR gate. So for OR gate we have in the first time interval and notice here

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:25.590
I didn't really specify what is the duration of each interval. Two zeros are 0 and in the

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:35.790
second interval we have at least one 1 which is here. So here the output is 1, 0 and 1

00:02:35.790 --> 00:02:48.710
is 1 and 1 and 1 is 1. Now when we have a little bit more complex circuit as the one

00:02:48.710 --> 00:02:55.990
given below, we first need to do some prep work. And prep work is we have to annotate

00:02:55.990 --> 00:03:02.550
the output of each gate. So we are going to start from node gate, output of node gate

00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:10.070
is going to be assigned a variable p, output on the top end gate we are going to call it q,

00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:18.260
and on the bottom one we are going to call r. Knowing that, we are going to go and start

00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:25.940
building our timing diagram. So I already assumed, showed that there are some values for a and b.

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:31.940
Let's look at now each part of the, sorry, each point of the

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:41.780
diagram that we just annotated, basically output of each gate. What are the values for p, q, and

00:03:41.780 --> 00:03:54.300
R. Let's look first for P. P is related to A, because P is not A. So in the first portion

00:03:54.300 --> 00:04:02.860
of my diagram, it is going to be 1. In the second portion of the diagram, where basically

00:04:02.860 --> 00:04:12.060
in second time interval is going to be 0, then it is going to be 1 and then 0, because

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:24.720
All the time that I'm drawing P, I'm actually looking at A because P is equal not A. Next

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:41.810
one is going to be Q. For Q, the inputs are P and B. So, let's change color. So, P and

00:04:41.810 --> 00:04:46.330
B are input for Q, zero and one.

00:04:48.050 --> 00:04:50.450
We can do this interval by interval,

00:04:50.450 --> 00:04:57.090
which is P, B, B is a zero.

00:04:57.090 --> 00:05:00.090
The moment something is zero and it's input to AND gate,

00:05:00.090 --> 00:05:01.370
it's going to be zero.

00:05:02.690 --> 00:05:07.210
Or we can look into the timing diagrams and say,

00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:12.010
is there a place in this timing diagram

00:05:12.010 --> 00:05:23.060
where both b and p are equal to 1. And that is this timing interval here. So before that

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:35.300
and after that all is equal to zero. Please feel free to go interval by interval if that is

00:05:35.300 --> 00:05:49.420
prefer the way for you. Let's look at R. What is R dependent on? R is A and B.

00:05:49.420 --> 00:06:18.000
Okay, so R, we're looking at a and b. 0 and 0 is 0. 0 and 1 is 0. 0 and 1 is again 0.

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:45.300
And final interval, 1 and 1 is 1. And y, so y is, sorry, dark green, y is output on OR gate,

00:06:45.300 --> 00:07:05.830
And OR is between Q and R. So OR gate is 1 at any moment when either of the inputs are

00:07:05.830 --> 00:07:22.870
So 0 or 0 is 0, 0 and or 0 is 0, 1 or 0 is 1, 1 or 0 is 1. So our final timing

00:07:22.870 --> 00:07:31.790
diagram has quite a few different values shown. So we have values on the inputs

00:07:31.790 --> 00:07:39.260
some what let's call them intermediate values which are the values in certain

00:07:39.260 --> 00:07:48.380
points of the circuit and then output values. Please do also read the chapter

00:07:48.380 --> 00:07:52.700
basic circuits drawing conventions because you will be needing it

00:07:52.700 --> 00:07:58.420
throughout the course and some of that we already have used here and that is for

00:07:58.420 --> 00:08:17.400
example and this point that we have let me just delete this at this point that

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:28.740
we have big dot and connection here and from A we have big dot but we cross

00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:36.110
these lines but that doesn't mean that they are connected, they're next to

00:08:36.110 --> 00:08:43.850
each other and you will see that that is shown in the chapter.

